-- MRCP Part 2: Cardiology (batch 1)
-- 50 questions covering acute coronary syndromes, arrhythmias, heart failure & cardiomyopathy,
-- hypertension, infective endocarditis, pericardial disease, valvular & structural heart disease,
-- and vascular & cardiac emergencies.

  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 62-year-old man with a background of type 2 diabetes and prior PCI to the LAD presents with 3 hours of central chest pain. ECG shows 3mm ST elevation in II, III and aVF with reciprocal depression in I and aVL. He is taken for primary PCI to the RCA within 90 minutes with a good angiographic result and TIMI 3 flow. On day 2, he develops recurrent chest pain with new ST elevation in the same territory and hypotension (BP 88/56 mmHg). Bedside echocardiography shows new inferior wall akinesis and a small pericardial effusion without tamponade features.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate next step?',
  array['reinfarction','stent thrombosis','post-PCI chest pain']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'mrcp2', '2ebe3b03-4042-4b85-ba76-83d16816f645',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 71-year-old woman presents with NSTEMI (troponin I 4500 ng/L, dynamic ECG changes with T wave inversion in V1-V4). She has stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73m2) and a GRACE score of 168. She is loaded with aspirin and ticagrelor and started on fondaparinux. The cardiology team plans an early invasive strategy.',
  'Which anticoagulant strategy is most appropriate to use as periprocedural cover at the time of angiography?',
  array['NSTEMI','renal impairment','periprocedural anticoagulation']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'mrcp2', '2ebe3b03-4042-4b85-ba76-83d16816f645',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 58-year-old man is 4 days post-anterior STEMI treated with primary PCI to a proximal LAD lesion (drug-eluting stent, TIMI 3 flow). He is established on aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin, ramipril and bisoprolol. He remains well, with an LVEF of 42% on pre-discharge echocardiogram and no arrhythmia on telemetry.',
  'What is the most appropriate duration of dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus ticagrelor) to plan for this patient?',
  array['DAPT duration','post-STEMI secondary prevention','drug-eluting stent']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'mrcp2', '2ebe3b03-4042-4b85-ba76-83d16816f645',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 68-year-old woman presents with an inferior STEMI and undergoes primary PCI to a dominant RCA. During the procedure she develops complete heart block with a junctional escape rhythm at 38 bpm and a systolic BP of 78 mmHg despite atropine 3 mg. Right ventricular involvement is confirmed on ECG (ST elevation in V4R) and echocardiography shows RV dysfunction.',
  'In addition to temporary pacing, which of the following is most appropriate in the immediate management of her hypotension?',
  array['inferior STEMI','right ventricular infarction','cardiogenic shock']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'mrcp2', '2ebe3b03-4042-4b85-ba76-83d16816f645',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 55-year-old man with a background of well-controlled hypertension presents with an acute anterior STEMI and undergoes successful primary PCI to the LAD within 2 hours of symptom onset. Coronary angiography of the non-culprit vessels reveals a moderate 60% stenosis in the mid-RCA, which is not intervened upon during the index procedure. He is haemodynamically stable post-procedure with no ongoing ischaemia.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to the non-culprit RCA lesion?',
  array['complete revascularisation','non-culprit lesion','staged PCI']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'mrcp2', '2ebe3b03-4042-4b85-ba76-83d16816f645',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 49-year-old woman with no significant past medical history presents with severe central chest pain radiating to the back, occurring shortly after a stressful family argument. ECG shows anterior ST elevation and troponin is significantly raised. Emergency coronary angiography shows no obstructive coronary disease. Left ventriculography demonstrates apical ballooning with basal hyperkinesis. She remains haemodynamically stable.',
  'Which of the following best describes the most appropriate next step in her management?',
  array['Takotsubo cardiomyopathy','stress cardiomyopathy','MINOCA']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'mrcp2', '6df5c231-68f7-4b21-9d31-ee12974fe5d8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 74-year-old man with permanent atrial fibrillation on apixaban and a background of ischaemic cardiomyopathy (LVEF 30%) is admitted with worsening breathlessness. His ventricular rate is persistently 130-150 bpm despite bisoprolol 10mg and digoxin at a therapeutic level. Echocardiography confirms reduced systolic function with no significant valve disease. He remains symptomatic with rate-related cardiomyopathy suspected.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['rate control failure','AV node ablation','cardiac resynchronisation therapy']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'mrcp2', '6df5c231-68f7-4b21-9d31-ee12974fe5d8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 39-year-old woman with structurally normal heart presents with recurrent palpitations. A 12-lead ECG during an episode shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 180 bpm with pseudo-R' waves in V1 and pseudo-S waves in the inferior leads. It terminates abruptly with IV adenosine. She wishes to avoid long-term medication if possible.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate definitive management?',
  array['AVNRT','catheter ablation','supraventricular tachycardia']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'mrcp2', '6df5c231-68f7-4b21-9d31-ee12974fe5d8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 67-year-old man with ischaemic cardiomyopathy (LVEF 25%) and a primary-prevention ICD in situ presents with multiple appropriate shocks over the past 24 hours for recurrent episodes of monomorphic ventricular tachycardia. He remains haemodynamically stable between episodes. He is already on maximally tolerated bisoprolol and has been loaded with IV amiodarone, but VT recurs.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management of this electrical storm?',
  array['electrical storm','VT ablation','refractory ventricular arrhythmia']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'mrcp2', '6df5c231-68f7-4b21-9d31-ee12974fe5d8',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 76-year-old woman with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a prior TIA (CHA2DS2-VASc score 6) has been established on apixaban 5mg twice daily for two years. She now requires elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic gallstones, classified as a standard-bleeding-risk procedure.',
  'What is the most appropriate perioperative management of her anticoagulation?',
  array['perioperative anticoagulation','DOAC interruption','bridging']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'mrcp2', '6df5c231-68f7-4b21-9d31-ee12974fe5d8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 45-year-old man is investigated for recurrent syncope. Resting ECG shows a coved-type ST elevation in V1-V2 with T wave inversion, and this pattern becomes more pronounced during a febrile illness. His brother died suddenly aged 38 with no cause found at post-mortem. Echocardiogram and coronary angiogram are normal.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in his management?',
  array['Brugada syndrome','sudden cardiac death risk','ICD']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'mrcp2', '6df5c231-68f7-4b21-9d31-ee12974fe5d8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 58-year-old man with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation is started on flecainide for a 'pill-in-the-pocket' rhythm control strategy. He has no structural heart disease on echocardiogram and normal coronary arteries on CT coronary angiography. Six months later he re-presents with a broad-complex tachycardia at 150 bpm, regular, with a sawtooth-like appearance and 1:1 AV conduction, causing presyncope.$x$,
  'What is the most likely explanation for this presentation, and what should be avoided in future management?',
  array['flecainide-induced atrial flutter','1:1 AV conduction','class Ic antiarrhythmics']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'mrcp2', '3c307550-8d07-4fbf-b41d-51e666130165',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 64-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (LVEF 28%, NYHA class II-III, ischaemic aetiology) remains symptomatic despite being established on sacubitril-valsartan, bisoprolol at target dose, spironolactone 25mg, and dapagliflozin. His most recent ECG shows sinus rhythm at 68 bpm with a QRS duration of 168ms and left bundle branch branch morphology. His renal function and potassium are stable.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in his management?',
  array['cardiac resynchronisation therapy','HFrEF','quadruple therapy']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'mrcp2', '3c307550-8d07-4fbf-b41d-51e666130165',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 71-year-old woman with HFrEF (LVEF 25%) on optimal quadruple therapy is admitted with acute decompensated heart failure. She is treated with IV furosemide with good diuretic response and clinical improvement. Pre-discharge bloods show potassium 4.3 mmol/L and eGFR 58 mL/min/1.73m2. She is currently on dapagliflozin 10mg daily, which was started as an outpatient 3 months ago.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to her SGLT2 inhibitor during this admission?',
  array['SGLT2 inhibitor','acute decompensated heart failure','in-hospital initiation']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'mrcp2', '3c307550-8d07-4fbf-b41d-51e666130165',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 52-year-old man is diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy (LVEF 30%) after presenting with breathlessness. Family history reveals his father died suddenly aged 45, and his sister has a pacemaker for heart block in her 30s. Genetic testing identifies a pathogenic LMNA mutation. He is currently NYHA class II with no documented arrhythmia.',
  'Which of the following most appropriately reflects the implication of this genetic finding for his management?',
  array['LMNA cardiomyopathy','sudden cardiac death risk','genetic risk stratification']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'mrcp2', '3c307550-8d07-4fbf-b41d-51e666130165',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 68-year-old man with known hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (maximal wall thickness 22mm) reports worsening exertional breathlessness and presyncope on exertion over the past 3 months. Echocardiography shows a resting left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) gradient of 85 mmHg with systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve and moderate mitral regurgitation. He is currently on bisoprolol 5mg once daily with ongoing symptoms.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['hypertrophic cardiomyopathy','LVOT obstruction','septal reduction therapy']
),
