  'draft', 'core',
  'A 56-year-old man of Black African ethnicity is newly diagnosed with hypertension (clinic BP 168/98 mmHg, confirmed on ambulatory monitoring with average daytime BP 162/96 mmHg). He has no diabetes and no proteinuria. He is otherwise fit and well.',
  'What is the most appropriate first-line antihypertensive therapy according to current guidance?',
  array['first-line antihypertensive','ethnicity-based prescribing','calcium channel blocker']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000023', 'mrcp2', 'b82172f5-bfe3-485e-a22f-2f58afbd1396',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 38-year-old woman presents with episodic headaches, palpitations, and profuse sweating, associated with paroxysmal severe hypertension (readings up to 220/130 mmHg during episodes, normal between episodes). 24-hour urinary metanephrines are markedly elevated. CT abdomen shows a 3.5cm right adrenal mass. She is scheduled for laparoscopic adrenalectomy in 3 weeks.',
  'What is the most appropriate preoperative pharmacological preparation?',
  array['phaeochromocytoma','preoperative alpha-blockade','surgical preparation']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000024', 'mrcp2', 'b82172f5-bfe3-485e-a22f-2f58afbd1396',
  'draft', 'advanced',
