  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 34-year-old woman presents with her third episode of pericarditis in 18 months, each responding initially to NSAIDs and colchicine but recurring on tapering therapy. She has no evidence of an underlying systemic disease on autoimmune and infective screening. Her current episode is confirmed with pleuritic chest pain, widespread saddle-shaped ST elevation, and a pericardial rub. She is now on her second recurrence despite adequate colchicine dosing and NSAID courses, with corticosteroids having been trialled and tapering attempts failing twice.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['recurrent pericarditis','IL-1 inhibition','corticosteroid-dependent pericarditis']
),
('d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000032', 'mrcp2', '5d442bd8-b127-4db3-bcf2-e9e7359190e1',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 58-year-old man presents with progressive ankle swelling, abdominal distension and exertional breathlessness over several months, 2 years after mediastinal radiotherapy for lymphoma. Examination reveals elevated JVP with a prominent y descent, Kussmaul's sign, and a pericardial knock. Echocardiography shows a thickened pericardium with septal bounce and preserved biventricular systolic function. Right and left heart catheterisation shows equalisation of diastolic pressures with a 'dip-and-plateau' pattern.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate definitive management?',
  array['constrictive pericarditis','post-radiotherapy','pericardiectomy']
),
